From: postmarketOS rhodep port
Subject: [DIAG] ASoC: q6asm-dai: probe whether the DSP answers RUN at all

The DSP acknowledges every ASM command it is sent, with status 0, and never
acknowledges ASM_SESSION_CMD_RUN_V2. That looked like the anomaly, but it
proves nothing on its own: RUN is sent from the PCM trigger, which runs in
atomic context, so it has to go out through q6asm_run_nowait(), and
downstream encodes an explicit no_wait flag in the RUN token via
q6asm_update_token() where mainline only sends the session id. A firmware
that stays silent for a nowait RUN would be behaving correctly.

So ask the question directly, from prepare(), where sleeping is allowed:
send one synchronous RUN and log what comes back. q6asm_ac_send_cmd_sync()
waits five seconds, so -ETIMEDOUT means the DSP is ignoring RUN outright,
while 0 or a DSP error status means RUN is being received and considered,
and the silence in the trigger path is just the nowait contract.

Diagnostic only.
--- a/sound/soc/qcom/qdsp6/q6asm-dai.c	2026-08-12 22:52:01.344301000 +0000
+++ b/sound/soc/qcom/qdsp6/q6asm-dai.c	2026-08-12 22:52:01.352301000 +0000
@@ -310,6 +310,20 @@
 
 	prtd->run_sent = false;
 
+	/* DIAG (not for upstream): the DSP answers every ASM command except
+	 * RUN, which it never acknowledges. RUN is normally sent from the
+	 * trigger, which is atomic, so it has to go out nowait and an absent
+	 * reply proves nothing. Send one synchronously from here, where
+	 * sleeping is allowed, purely to find out whether the DSP answers it
+	 * at all: a 5 second timeout means it is ignoring RUN outright.
+	 */
+	if (substream->stream == SNDRV_PCM_STREAM_PLAYBACK) {
+		int probe = q6asm_run(prtd->audio_client, prtd->stream_id,
+				      0, 0, 0);
+
+		ASM_DIAG("probe: synchronous RUN rc=%d", probe);
+	}
+
 	return ret;
 
